# blackhole-and-dark-matter.otd — OTD7.2
#
# Two new things, both built on real physics rather than a "dense mixed
# matter" stand-in for dark matter, or a "made of visible elements" stand-in
# for a black hole:
#
#   material: dark_matter  — real local density (~6e-22 kg/m³, about twenty
#                             orders of magnitude below hydrogen gas) and
#                             opacity 0.0 (zero electromagnetic interaction —
#                             fully invisible, not just faint). Its block
#                             below will report a mass so small it's
#                             effectively zero — that's the actual physics,
#                             not a bug: dark matter's gravity only adds up
#                             at the scale of a whole galactic halo.
#
#   simulate: blackhole     — takes the heaviest part in the scene and asks
#                             "how small would this exact mass need to be
#                             compressed to become a black hole?" (its
#                             Schwarzschild radius), then reports what that
#                             hypothetical hole's photon sphere, innermost
#                             stable orbit, and Hawking temperature would be.
#
# Try it:
#   otd --check examples/blackhole-and-dark-matter.otd

scene "A tungsten sphere, and the dark matter you can't see next to it"

# the heaviest thing in the scene — everyday-dense, not remotely close to
# collapsing, which is exactly the point: `simulate: blackhole` reports how
# absurdly far from a black hole any buildable object actually is
core = sphere(r: 15cm) at (0, 15cm, 0) material: tungsten

# invisible on purpose (opacity: 0.0) — you'll see almost nothing rendered
# here, and its reported mass will round to zero at this scale
halo = cube(w: 2m, d: 2m, h: 2m) at (0, 1m, 0) material: dark_matter

simulate: blackhole
